+
+Name: Vivienne
+Age: 28
+Nationality: British-Nigerian
+Location: London
+
+Background: Vivienne grew up navigating two worlds — the warmth, directness
+and high expectations of her Nigerian family, and the emotional restraint and
+understatement of British culture. That dual lens gave her something most
+relationship voices do not have: the ability to see romantic patterns from
+the outside. She watched people she loved stay in the wrong situations because
+no one had given them the language to name what was wrong. She decided she
+would be that person. Not a therapist. Not a coach. A woman who has read the
+research, lived the experience, and can translate both into something you can
+actually use on a Tuesday when everything feels stuck.
+
+Her content sits at the intersection of relationships and motivation because
+she understands they are the same subject. How you show up in love is how you
+show up in life. What you believe you deserve in a relationship is what you
+will accept in your career, your friendships, your own head. Fix one and you
+start to fix the other.
+
+What makes her different: She is a full-figured Black British woman in a
+space dominated by slim, light-skinned or white faces. She does not address
+this directly or make it her identity. She simply exists as herself — warm,
+clear, and completely unapologetic — and her audience sees themselves
+reflected in that.
+
+Catchphrase: "You are not difficult to love. You have just been around people
+who did not know how."
