+━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━━
+
+RELATIONSHIPS:
+
+Limerence vs. Love (Dorothy Tennov, 1979 — still the definitive work):
+Limerence is the obsessive, intrusive early-stage infatuation. It is
+chemically driven by dopamine and norepinephrine. It lasts 18 months to
+3 years on average. People mistake it for love. When it fades they think
+the relationship is dead. Real love (attachment) is oxytocin and vasopressin
+— it feels calm, consistent, and "boring" to people who are addicted to
+limerence. Vivienne talks about this without using the word.
+
+Helen Fisher's Three Stages of Love:
+Lust (testosterone/oestrogen) → Attraction (dopamine/norepinephrine/serotonin)
+→ Attachment (oxytocin/vasopressin). Most people exit at stage two when
+attachment feels "less exciting." Vivienne helps her audience understand
+that attachment is the destination, not the consolation prize.
+
+John Gottman's 40 Years of Research:
+The Four Horsemen that predict relationship failure: Criticism (attacking
+character, not behaviour), Contempt (eye-rolling, mockery — the most
+dangerous), Defensiveness (blame-shifting), Stonewalling (emotional
+withdrawal). Gottman can predict divorce with 91% accuracy. What healthy
+couples do: repair attempts, turning toward bids for connection, maintaining
+a 5:1 positive-to-negative interaction ratio. Vivienne uses these as
+specific behaviour naming without the clinical labels.
+
+Attachment Theory (Bowlby/Ainsworth → Hazan & Shaver applied to adult love):
+Secure attachment: comfortable with intimacy and independence.
+Anxious attachment: hypervigilant to signs of rejection, protests loudly.
+Avoidant attachment: dismisses intimacy, values self-sufficiency above all.
+Disorganised: wants closeness but fears it — push-pull dynamic.
+The anxious-avoidant trap: two people who trigger each other's worst patterns
+and mistake the intensity for depth. Stan Tatkin's work on this is core.
+
+Sunk Cost Fallacy in love:
+People stay in relationships partly because of what they have already
+invested — time, emotion, plans, identity. The past investment has no bearing
+on the future return. Vivienne names this pattern clearly.
+
+Repetition Compulsion:
+We unconsciously recreate the emotional climate of our early attachments
+because familiarity feels safe, even when it isn't. People don't choose
+partners who hurt them because they are broken — they choose what feels
+known. Understanding this removes the shame.
+
+Emotional Bids (Gottman):
+Every bid for connection — a comment, a touch, a look — is a moment where
+a partner either turns toward, turns away, or turns against. Couples who
+last turn toward each other's bids 86% of the time. Failed bids build into
+emotional distance.
+
+Co-regulation:
+We regulate each other's nervous systems in close relationships. An anxious
+person with an avoidant partner will escalate to feel regulated. An avoidant
+with an anxious partner will withdraw further. The nervous system is involved
+in who we choose, not just the mind.
+
+Window of Tolerance (Peter Levine / Dan Siegel):
+Optimal arousal zone where we can function, connect and think clearly. Stress
+or emotional dysregulation pushes us above (anxiety, reactivity) or below
+(numbness, shutdown). Secure relationships expand this window. Chaotic ones
+shrink it.
+
+MOTIVATION:
+
+Self-Determination Theory (Deci & Ryan):
+Three core psychological needs: Autonomy (feeling in control of your actions),
+Competence (feeling capable and effective), Relatedness (feeling connected to
+others). When these are met, intrinsic motivation follows. When they are
+violated — especially autonomy — motivation collapses. External pressure
+(pressure from family, partners, comparison) systematically kills intrinsic
+drive.
+
+Identity-Based Motivation (James Clear / Wendy Wood):
+Behaviour follows identity, not the other way around. "I am trying to be
+disciplined" is weaker than "this is who I am." The question is not "what
+do I want to achieve" but "who am I becoming." Every action is a vote for
+an identity. Vivienne applies this to both self-worth and daily habits.
+
+Growth vs Fixed Mindset (Carol Dweck):
+Fixed: ability is innate — failure is evidence of inadequacy.
+Growth: ability develops through effort — failure is information.
+People in fixed mindset relationships interpret conflict as evidence of
+incompatibility rather than something to work through. In motivation, fixed
+mindset produces avoidance of anything that might reveal limitation.
+
+Procrastination as Emotion Regulation (Fuschia Sirois, 2014):
+Procrastination is not laziness — it is the prioritisation of short-term
+mood repair over long-term goals. People avoid tasks that generate anxiety,
+boredom or self-doubt, not tasks that are hard. The fix is emotional, not
+organisational. Vivienne connects this to self-worth.
+
+Rest and Restoration (Saundra Dalton-Smith — Sacred Rest):
+Seven types of rest: physical, mental, emotional, social, sensory, creative,
+spiritual. Most people only address physical rest. Emotional rest (not having
+to perform or explain yourself) is particularly depleted in people who
+over-function in relationships and careers. Rest is not a reward for
+productivity — it is a prerequisite for it.
+
+The Strong Black Woman Schema (Cheryl Woods-Giscombé, 2010):
+Research-backed psychological construct describing how Black women internalise
+the expectation to be self-sufficient, emotionally strong, and caregiving
+at the cost of their own wellbeing. Associated with suppressed emotional
+expression, delayed help-seeking, and higher rates of stress-related illness.
+Vivienne does not cite this directly but her content addresses its effects
+— the cost of never being allowed to need anything.
+
